+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | Shipped              |
+
+`ToolRegistry` hands every declared `inputSchema` to the providers verbatim, but nothing checked what the model actually sends back: `execute` looked the tool up, logged the input's key names and called its executor whatever the input carried. This spec pins the contract that closes that gap - `execute` validates the input against the resolved definition's schema before the executor runs, and a call the schema rejects becomes a `ToolResponse` the model can read and correct, not an exception that ends the conversation.
+
+## What execute enforces
+
+- A call whose input fails the resolved tool definition's `inputSchema` is rejected inside `execute` before the executor runs: the return value is a `ToolResponse` whose `result` describes what was wrong, and the executor is never invoked ([validated by: rejects a call missing a required property before the executor runs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L42)).
+- Validation covers `required`, declared property types and declared `enum` values, and nothing else: a valid call whose input carries a property the schema does not list reaches the executor exactly as sent - no default injected, no type coerced - and exactly once ([validated by: passes valid input with an unlisted property through to the executor unchanged, exactly once](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L79)).
+- A missing `required` property is rejected with a message naming the property as required ([validated by: rejects a call missing a required property before the executor runs](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L42)).
+- A property whose value is the wrong declared type is rejected with a message naming the property path and the expected type, and the message carries no part of the offending value ([validated by: rejects a wrong-typed property naming the path and expected type without echoing the value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L54)).
+- A value outside a declared `enum` is rejected with a message naming that property ([validated by: rejects a value outside a declared enum naming the property](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L67)).
+
+## Schema compilation
+
+- `register` compiles a static definition's schema once at registration time, so a schema that cannot compile throws from `register` - naming the tool - rather than failing mid-conversation ([validated by: throws at registration time for a schema that cannot compile, naming the tool](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L93)).
+- A schema produced by a function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved per call, so a source whose returned schema changes between calls is validated against the schema current at that call, never a stale one ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema current at each call](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L109)).
+
+## Redaction
+
+- Neither a rejection's returned text nor any log line `execute` writes carries a property value from the rejected payload - property paths and the constraint that failed only, because tool input is where identifying data enters the engine and the engine redacts nothing today ([validated by: carries no property value from a rejected payload into the log lines or the returned text](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L137)).
+
+## Unknown tool
+
+- An unregistered name still throws `Unknown tool: <name>` from `execute`, deliberately unchanged by validation ([validated by: still throws for an unregistered tool name](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L151)).
+
+## The tool loop
+
+- A rejected tool call reaches the model as an ordinary tool result - `processMessage` resolves rather than rejecting, and the messages the provider receives on the next round carry the rejection text, so a corrected retry fits inside the existing `DEFAULT_MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS` budget instead of surfacing as a generic transport error ([validated by: feeds a rejected tool call back to the provider as a tool result and resolves on the corrected retry](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts#L57)).
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Validating tool *output* - this spec pins input only.
+- Replacing the `Unknown tool: <name>` throw with a returned tool result: the same failure class handled the opposite way, an intentional split tracked as its own issue.
+- Hallucination detection and an evaluation harness; per-tool authorization, since a schema-valid call for someone else's data is still valid and authorization is a separate `ToolContext` boundary concern no issue in this epic owns; retry, timeout and rate limiting around tool execution; and any actual tool implementation, written against this contract later.
